;;; `std.slots` — `slot-adjacent-bwd-neg`, and the **direction** of its
;;; structure premise. M1e S1e.3.6 T6, and the fixture that kills the survivor
;;; `tests/README.md` had recorded since M1c.
;;;
;;; ── What this is for ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The mutation sweep (`utils/stdlib_mutants.py`) exchanges the two arguments
;;; of one premise at a time and asks whether `ein test tests/` notices. On
;;;
;;;   (rule slot-adjacent-bwd-neg (?R ?S ?isa ?PT)
;;;     :match  (and (?S ?a ?b)
;;;                  (not (?R ?b ?p1)) (?isa ?p1 ?PT)
;;;                  (?S ?p2 ?p1) …            ←  this one
;;;
;;; the exchange `(?S ?p1 ?p2)` **survived**: `08_spatial_adjacent.ein` needs
;;; the rule, but on a three-seat row the exclusion it should derive is
;;; reachable from the other clue's chain a pass later, so the suite still
;;; passed. A mutation score with an unnamed survivor is a slogan, and one with
;;; a *named* survivor is a debt.
;;;
;;; ── Why the exchanged rule is not merely wrong but silent ──────────────────
;;;
;;; It is worth knowing that this mutant derives nothing **false** — which is
;;; why no ordinary contradiction fixture could ever have caught it. The guard
;;;
;;;   (absent (and (?S ?p2 ?p_o) (?isa ?p_o ?PT) (neq ?p_o ?p1)))
;;;
;;; asks that `?p1` be the only `?S`-source of `?p2`. With the premise
;;; exchanged, `?p1` is `?p2`'s *successor*, so the guard demands that `?p2`'s
;;; only predecessor be its own successor — impossible on a row, and vacuously
;;; true exactly when `?p2` has no predecessor at all. There the conclusion
;;; *`?a` is not at `?p2`* is already true for a different reason (`?a` is
;;; right of `?b`, so it is never at the leftmost seat), and `slot-endpoint-fwd`
;;; says so. The exchanged rule is therefore **sound and strictly weaker**: it
;;; can only be caught by a program that *needs* the derivation it stops
;;; making.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   right-of:  S1 ← S2 ← S3 ← S4
;;;   the clue:  (right-of Ann Cat)   — Ann's seat is immediately right of Cat's
;;;   the input: ¬(co-loc Cat S3)
;;;
;;; `slot-adjacent-bwd-neg` fires with `?b = Cat`, `?p1 = S3`, `?p2 = S4` —
;;; S4's only `right-of`-source is S3, so the guard holds — and concludes
;;; **¬(co-loc Ann S4)**: Ann at S4 would put Cat at S3, which is excluded.
;;;
;;; **A fourth seat is what makes it isolable.** On three seats the same
;;; conclusion is one elimination step away; here Ann still has S2 and S3, so
;;; nothing else has decided her domain when the rule fires, and the exchanged
;;; rule's silence shows.
;;;
;;; ── How the expectation sees a negative ────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Stored negatives are the one thing an `:expect` does not close (S1c.1.2's
;;; rule 3), so *"the exclusion was derived"* cannot be said by listing the
;;; exclusions that were. `probe-excluded` turns the one that matters into an
;;; ordinary positive the expectation can close — `07_schroder.ein`'s idiom
;;; with the polarity reversed: there `(unknown …)` proves a negative was
;;; **not** derived, here a `(not …)` premise proves one **was**. Reading a
;;; stored negative as a premise is legitimate and is the distinction ein's
;;; three states exist for; reading its *absence* would not be.
;;;
;;; Priority 500 for the same reason the probe there has it: something that
;;; reads the world has to be the last thing to read it.
;;;
;;; The verdict is `Open` — the seating is not determined and `co-loc` owes
;;; sixteen placements — and that is deliberate. What is being tested is one
;;; derivation, not a puzzle, and the claim is about the fact set the state
;;; reached, which is what an `:expect` is about however the read-out words it.

(import std.algebra :symbols (symmetric symmetric-negative-setup))
(import std.slots   :symbols (slot-partition-setup slot-spatial-setup))

(relation type     T T :why "{?1} is a subtype of {?2}")
(relation instance T T :why "{?1} is an instance of {?2}")
(relation co-loc   Attribute Attribute :why "{?1} and {?2} are in the same seat")
(relation right-of Attribute Attribute :why "{?1}'s seat is immediately right of {?2}'s")
(relation excluded Attribute Attribute :why "{?1} was ruled out of {?2}")

(symmetric co-loc)
(slot-partition co-loc instance type Attribute Seat)
(slot-spatial   co-loc right-of instance Seat)

(type Attribute T)
(type Seat Attribute) (type Who Attribute) (type Pet Attribute)

(instance S1 Seat) (instance S2 Seat) (instance S3 Seat) (instance S4 Seat)
(instance Ann Who) (instance Bob Who) (instance Cid Who) (instance Dee Who)
(instance Cat Pet) (instance Dog Pet) (instance Rat Pet) (instance Owl Pet)

;; Four seats in a row.
(right-of S2 S1) (right-of S3 S2) (right-of S4 S3)

;; One clue, started by an exclusion rather than a placement.
(right-of Ann Cat) (not (co-loc Cat S3))

(rule probe-excluded ()
  :match  (not (co-loc Ann S4))
  :assert (excluded Ann S4)
  :priority 500
  :why    "slot-adjacent-bwd-neg reached ¬(co-loc Ann S4) from ¬(co-loc Cat S3)")

(query
  :goal (excluded ?x ?y)
  :no-hypothesis (type instance co-loc right-of excluded)
  :expect (model (excluded Ann S4)))
